WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.060
Multi-host administration increases both usefulness and blast radius.

2
00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:11.800
A script may run the same action across a host group, region, environment, or inventory file.

3
00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:15.980
That can save time, but it can also spread a mistake quickly.

4
00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:19.460
The script should confirm the target set before execution.

5
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:24.440
Operators should know which hosts are included, which are excluded, and why.

6
00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:30.220
For unattended jobs, logs should record the selected scope in a safe high-level way.

7
00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:32.020
Concurrency controls matter.

8
00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:40.600
Running too many remote actions at once can overload services, exhaust connections, hide failures in noisy output, or make rollback harder.

9
00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:45.580
Rate limits and batches help maintain control and make results easier to inspect.

10
00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:48.420
Partial failure handling should be explicit.

11
00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:54.600
Some hosts may be unreachable, already updated, missing prerequisites, or in a different state.

12
00:00:54.700 --> 00:01:01.660
The script should distinguish full success, partial success, expected skips, and failures that require operator attention.

13
00:01:01.940 --> 00:01:06.340
Idempotent and rerunnable behavior reduces recovery stress.

14
00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:12.280
A well-designed remote action can be safely repeated or resumed without compounding the problem.

15
00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:20.060
Where high-impact changes are involved, staged rollout or canary-style execution helps detect issues before every target is affected.

16
00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:23.700
The goal is controlled progress, not maximum speed.

17
00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:31.960
Remote administration should prioritize safe scope, understandable results, and recovery paths over the fastest possible fan-out.
